      <p class="route-row route-row--violet"><span>What earns trust</span>None.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--gold"><span>Watch out</span>Forgotten quickly.</p>
      <p class="route-row route-row--teal"><span>How it grows</span>Add tasks and follow-up.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path:</strong> organisers can build a small, repeatable programme with local professionals and a simple feedback loop, and run it regularly for several schools.
    </p>
    <p>
      Follow school rules and student safety policies.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sales">Who pays for these skills, and how they judge you</h2>

  <p>
    Schools and families value sessions that lead to action. They judge organisers by clarity, fairness to students and follow-through.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first basic skill is designing one activity that ends with a written next step.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and running a career session: which tasks are under pressure</h2>

  <p>
    AI tools suggest careers from interests and summarise roles. Treat the output as a list to test, not an answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Self-knowledge, real trials and decisions you own hold value. A staged path helps: now, use AI to generate options; later, test them with people and small projects.
  </p>

  <h2 id="better">Alternatives that can beat a general running a career session path</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Alternative</th>
          <th>Why it can beat it</th>
          <th>Catch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Single large seminar</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Wide reach</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Little follow-up</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Small workshop series</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Deeper work</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">More effort</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Alternative"><strong>Shadow days</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why it can beat it">Real exposure</td>
          <td data-label="Catch">Needs partners</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="learn">How to learn running a career session cheaply, and what proof counts</h2>

  <p>
    Running a career session is learned by doing real work, so the cheapest route is also the best. A paid course is worth it only when it gives feedback and finished work that you cannot get free.
  </p>
  <h3>Free and low-cost routes first</h3>
  <ul class="check-list">
    <li>Free worksheets and activity templates.</li>
    <li>Free public notices for real examples.</li>
    <li>Free feedback form tools.</li>
    <li>A colleague who watches a session and gives feedback.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>How to judge a paid course</h3>
  <p>
    Judge each course, not the platform. Ask what you will build, who reviews it, which role graduates reached, how current the material is and what the refund terms are, in writing.
  </p>
  <h3>Your week, and a first small test</h3>
  <p>
    A starting plan is about ten hours a week: three of lessons and seven of practice on one project. Do not pay for an expensive programme before this habit holds.
  </p>
  <p>
    This month, design one ninety-minute session with a follow-up and test it with a small group.
  </p>
  <h3>Building a high-income skill portfolio beside the credential</h3>
  <p>
    A degree has a place, and the weak spot is relying on it alone. A skill mix of one core skill, a second skill, communication, a niche and a clear offer builds a high-income skill portfolio, and a niche service can bring an earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <h2 id="plan">A ninety-minute session plan</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Minutes</th>
          <th>Activity</th>
          <th>Output</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Minutes"><strong>0 to 10</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Activity">Welcome and one question: what do you enjoy doing most?</td>
          <td data-label="Output">A quick poll of the room</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Minutes"><strong>10 to 30</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Activity">Short talk on how to test options, with two real examples</td>
          <td data-label="Output">Notes for students</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Minutes"><strong>30 to 55</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Activity">Small-group activity: cluster interests and pick one trial</td>
          <td data-label="Output">A group sheet</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Minutes"><strong>55 to 75</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Activity">Two short talks by professionals on a normal day in their work</td>
          <td data-label="Output">Questions list</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Minutes"><strong>75 to 90</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Activity">Each student writes one trial and a date</td>
          <td data-label="Output">A one-line plan</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="brief">A speaker brief that gets useful talks</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Ask the speaker to cover</th>
          <th>Why</th>
          <th>Avoid</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Ask the speaker to cover"><strong>A normal day, not highlights</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why">Realism</td>
          <td data-label="Avoid">Only success stories</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Ask the speaker to cover"><strong>What they would test before choosing again</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why">Practical advice</td>
          <td data-label="Avoid">Generic motivation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Ask the speaker to cover"><strong>One mistake and what it taught</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why">Honesty</td>
          <td data-label="Avoid">Hiding failures</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Ask the speaker to cover"><strong>Entry routes and costs</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why">Decision information</td>
          <td data-label="Avoid">Brand promotion</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Ask the speaker to cover"><strong>Five minutes for questions</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Why">Interaction</td>
          <td data-label="Avoid">Long monologues</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="followup">Feedback form and follow-up that make it count</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tool</th>
          <th>Questions or steps</th>
          <th>Use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Tool"><strong>Exit slip</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions or steps">What is one thing you will try this month?</td>
          <td data-label="Use">Collect before students leave</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Tool"><strong>Feedback form</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions or steps">What was most useful? What was unclear? What would you add?</td>
          <td data-label="Use">Improve the next session</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Tool"><strong>One-week follow-up</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions or steps">Send each student a reminder of their trial</td>
          <td data-label="Use">Turns talk into action</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Tool"><strong>One-month check</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Questions or steps">Ask what happened</td>
          <td data-label="Use">Shows impact</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checkpoint">The 4-Checkpoint Protocol for this career</h2>

  <p>
    Test the choice with <strong>The 4-Checkpoint Protocol</strong>: Biology, Context, Market and Survival.
  </p>
  <div class="protocol">
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Biology: what will students do differently after your session?</div>
        <p>Write one measurable action.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Context: time, money and home support</div>
        <p>Count time, money and support at home.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Market: who hires?</div>
        <p>Read ten notices for each option you are weighing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="protocol-row">
      <div class="protocol-row__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="protocol-row__title">Survival: what survives AI?</div>
        <p>Lists of options are cheap. Tested choices hold value.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="gate3">The 3 Gates: proof before commitment</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>The 3 Gates</strong> check that your effort turns into work other people rely on.
  </p>
  <div class="gates-block">
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of design</span>
        <p>Share the plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of feedback</span>
        <p>Share form results.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of follow-up</span>
        <p>Share action counts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Expensive mistakes</h2>

  <div class="mistake-list">
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">01</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Lecture only</div>
        <p>Add tasks.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">02</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Promotional speakers</div>
        <p>Brief them.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">03</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">No follow-up</div>
        <p>Plan the week after.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">04</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Too many topics</div>
        <p>Set one goal.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mistake">
      <div class="mistake__num">05</div>
      <div>
        <div class="mistake__title">Ignoring feedback</div>
        <p>Review each session.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    Pick one route, finish one project and show it to two people in the field. Then read ten live notices and apply or pitch a small client.
  </p>
  <p>
    A specialist can look at your strengths, finances and goals with you. A sensible first step is <a href="/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/student-career-guidance/">student career guidance</a>. The aim is a high-income skill portfolio and an earlier path to financial freedom.
  </p>

  <p>
    For a report outline and follow-up plan, read <a href="/blog/career-guidance/how-to-write-a-report-on-a-career-guidance-programme-in-school-india/">report on a career guidance programme</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    For what teachers can do and a term plan, read <a href="/blog/career-guidance/role-of-teachers-in-career-guidance-india/">role of teachers in career guidance</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
